I wanted  to ask obviously academics people who  study say the environment full-time in a  very serious way are among the most  informed people in our society on  whatever the topic is that they study is  there a role for what is the proper role  i guess i would ask in your view of  academics in the policy debate is there  is there a point at which they should be  careful not to become too involved in  the political aspect of it should they  remain exclusively advisers and  researchers or do you think that they  should be directly involved in taking  political stance on some of these issues  I think there's a this room for a  plurality of approaches and I think a  lot of that will depend on the  individual themselves and you know we  have seen great examples of individual  scientists getting involved and and and  actually producing meaningful policy  that change so I think that that is you  know there is a role for that but I  I think the key is to make sure that the  science and the analysis is right and is  solid and then if it is solid I think  that I think that that scientists have  that option and some may feel it's a  duty to make sure that people understand  the situation and I think that was the  case with sherry Rowland and and and the  ozone layer I think many of the  scientists involved in the climate  change debate feel they have a  responsibility to make the consequences  of their results clearly known and of  course that happens in many other areas  too but it's really important that the  science is well done peer reviewed  absolutely solid because once that once  that goes I think you are on very shaky  foundations let me take another question  from the viewers and this is from asiah  why is it so hard for developed  countries and there's a premise embedded  in this question why is it so hard for  developed countries to adopt  environmental protections and I guess I  would add to that you one might also ask  why is it so hard in in fully developed  countries to do that I think that you  know when you look back even in my  lifetime so the quality environment has  changed quite a bit and in some respects  has changed for the better in many  countries in the developed world because  they've understood the importance of  putting in pollution control measures  understood the importance of putting in  place the right kinds of policies so if  you look at air quality inn in not all  but many North American cities it's  better than it used to be certainly  that's the case in the UK if you look at  the water quality it's better than it  used to be and some developing countries  are on a similar trajectory but some  some way behind the developed world the  problems they're having with air  pollution in in Beijing right now very  reminiscent of the problems  london had in the 1950s so i'm sure that  that that they will get there but as the  chinese government thinks about these  things it's got many different  priorities to balance but i know for a  fact that they are putting in the right  kinds of environmental legislation but  it's takes some time and it's going to  take some time to get it in force just  as it just as it did in britain in the  1950s what is the your view the relative  the relative roles for government and  play industry and and perhaps even  individuals in addressing some of the  problems we face this is good government  be the primary driver should industry  should do we really need to take  individual responsibilities at the at  the individual level to make any  meaningful change i think i think  obviously all three have a  responsibility governments I think have  a responsibility to create the right  kind of policies that move society in a  sensible direction with regard to  environmental stewardship and  sustainability I think companies are  increasingly interested in these issues  for a whole variety of reasons not just  their license to operate and the and the  defensive posture of escaping  environmental regulation but also from  from the standpoint of ensuring  continuity of their business operations  ensuring the continuity of their their  raw materials so as these problems begin  to bite I think you will see and I think  you're already seeing greater interest  from the business community in these  environments I heard one chief executive  of a major u.s. corporation express it  this way a few months ago think about  the changing role of the sustainability  officer once the sustainability officer  was at the periphery just taking care of  a whole bunch of compliance issues many  companies now the sustainability officer  is actually at the center of the company  close to the chief executive advising  him or her on how the company should  respond to these environmental  another question this one from Scott and  this one is a little technical but i'll  ask anyway how will how will climate  change affect the spatial distance  between the Earth and the Sun or the  eccentricity of Earth's orbit around the  Sun I have to say this one I hadn't  contemplated before but well I think  what sort of being referred to there is  is if you look at the kind of macro  scale climatic change over the last  several million years it's very clear  that the sort of glacial and  interglacial cycles are driven by all  the changes in three key orbital  parameters but in it so there's there's  a clear role of planetary factors in  long-term climate change but what we're  seeing here is a totally different order  of a totally different time scale you  know we're seeing a rapid buildup of co2  in the atmosphere that has clear and  demonstrable changes and we're seeing it  happen extremely rapidly compared to  these 10,000 year hundred thousand years  sort of geological cycles so this is a  kind of a different problem but it's  it's certainly part of the overall  climate change picture but i think it's  not relevant to what's happening here  and now and has happened in the last 50  years or the last 200 years question  about education environmental education  there aren't that many schools that i  know of in the united states that are  have a free-standing schools of the  environment within them does that seem  to be changing our schools are we seeing  new schools of the environment crop up I  think that's an interesting question I  think we're very lucky here at Yale  because we had a forestry school that's  over a hundred years old and over time  that school is adapted and changed and  respond to a whole range of broader  environmental issues not solely forestry  now but but climate water energy policy  economics and and and so on so I think  we're very fortunate in that regard and  other universities that we certain  have some great environment schools at  other universities of Duke Michigan at  Santa Barbara and so on but at many  universities the environment sort of  falls in the interstices between  traditional departments and that I think  is a problem the geology department owns  a bit of this issue the biology  department owns a bit of this issue the  economics department owns a bit of this  issue so for many universe is the  challenge is how to put together a  credible environmental curriculum that  crosses multiple units with within the  academic institution and that's often  harder than it should be and I think for  a really integrated program to happen  that's good that's quite a that's quite  a challenge but but you are seeing  universities respond because first the  issues are central to society now and  second because their central to society  there's increased student demand  students are interested in these issues  student wants students want to learn  about these issues and so universities  somehow have to figure out how to  respond and it's not just students who  are interested in becoming climate  scientists either or even scientists at  all necessarily absolutely because as  I've said these issues affect every  aspect of society they're working their  way increasingly into every aspect of  our daily lives this is an important  issue for the future let's talk about a  project of yours you've recently  published a new book called ginkgo tell  us about the ginkgo tree and how you got  interested in it and why I decided to  write a book about it well this this in  a way was was kind of a little piece of  fun for me although it's it turned into  a to a much larger project than I'd  hoped it's about a particular tree and  but the book really in the book really  is a kind of a particular species of  tree particular species their  distinctive species of tree one of the  most common street trees in Asia and for  example here in in the Connecticut in  Manhattan it's one of the comments  street trees but it has a wonderful  story to tell it has a deep evolutionary  history it has a deep and complicated  cultural history so in a way the book is  an evolutionary and cultural biography  of a tree but at the same time it gives  me the opportunity to talk about some of  these environmental issues and to  reflect on them one of the questions  that it brings up is this whole issue of  time you know we've been talking about  time you know in my lifetime in a little  over 50 years the global population is  more than doubled its gone from three to  seven billion in the last 50 years so we  inevitably focus on these short time  frames but in the grand history of our  planet in the 4.6 billion year history  of our planet or even the 200 million  year plus history of the ginkgo tree  this is a short time span so part of  this book was to ask people particularly  the last few chapters as well ask people  to kind of reflect on time and to  reflect on our our brief 10 years brief  10 years on this planet and think about  the long view to try to take the long  view instead of the short view all of  the time now the correct me if I'm wrong  and I know you will ginkgo tree did it  almost get wiped out and then rebounded  yeah and another reason why I wanted to  write this story is that this is  actually a kind of a good news story and  once I was looking for us for something  that's a good news story I mean this  this was a tree that was widespread for  millions of years across our planet  lived on every continent but almost went  extinct over the last few million years  and exists today as wild populations  possibly in just two locations in in  China so in a way the fact that we still  have ginkgo around and the fact that we  now know it and we can see it and enjoy  it is really because of the relationship  of that tree to people and this it's  people that have spread that plant  around the world and I think it's kind  of fascinating  to think that all of those ginkgos that  you see on the campus here in New Haven  or in Manhattan or San Francisco or  Tokyo all come from those two wild  populations in China so in part the  cultural biography is about the human  relationship with this particular tree  and I think you can argue that it's a  species that humans have saved rather  than driven to extinction elaborate on  that over by spreading it by spreading  it and so obviously the most important  thing is to conserve species where they  live and where they grow in the natural  ecosystems of which they are part but I  think we're fortunate indeed to have  many plant species and many animal  species to still available for us to  love and understand and study because we  have learned how to cultivate them so  for me another message in this book is  that conservation through cultivation is  an important way that we will we will  look after plants in the future and  indeed you know this this is a broader  trend you know with so much of the  Earth's surface already dramatically  changed by people we have to find homes  for native plants and animals in  human-dominated landscapes and we often  need to intervene to make sure that  happens and that's the only way that  many of these species are going to  survive this reminds me of this brings  to mind urbanization again and of course  China is still developing very fast many  of the biggest cities of the future may  the biggest current cities are in China  but most of the Urban Development i'm  not mistaken anticipate in the next  century or so is expected in in asia and  china doesn't have the strongest  environmental record too to what extent  do you think there's room for the rest  of the world to bring the attention  bring make it a priority for the chinese  i think actually it's already a priority  for them but they're juggling multiple  priorities  and they understand the bird the  environmental burdens that they will  face if they can't get to grips with  these issues that they're facing the  challenge of a very large population so  I think that they understand but in a  way they have the same problems that we  do getting things done sometimes is a  problem he even here in the even here in  the US but I think there's a there's a  broader issue about urbanization that  I'd kind of like to mention it and that  is as the world's population now more  than fifty percent of the global  population lives in urban areas as that  happens I think a really important  question is to what extent will people  lose the connection that they had with  nature when they lived in rural  environments that may not have always  been a happy connection for them but  people were always aware of their  connect their connection to the natural  world think one of the bigger dangers in  in urbanization is that people lose that  sense of connection with the natural  world and therefore take it for granted  and you can see this in the smallest  ways where the kids in in the inner-city  don't know where their vegetables come  from or don't know where that milk comes  from but you also see it in the sense  that that do they care about the the  plants that are in their environment  what is their relationship to animals  how 